|> Exercise
    handle=_max_sinx_+_cosx_new_ver_

    The graph $y = \cos x + \sin x$ from Exercise 
    >>_four_graphs_ has a maximum value greater than $1$:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_maximum_of_sin_plus_cos.svg

    What is this maximum value, and for which
    value(s) of $x$ is it achieved?
    (Reason geometrically, NOT by calculus.)

    |> Solution

        Note that if we move 
        diagonally along a line of angle $-45^\circ$ 
        from a point $(x_1, y_1)$
        to a point $(x_2, y_2)$...

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_max_cosx_plus_sinx_x1_y1_x2_y2.svg

        ...then...

        $$
        x_1 + y_1 = x_2 + y_2
        $$

        ...because whatever has been gained in the $x$-coordinate
        has been lost in the $y$-coordinate, and vice-versa. In
        particular,

        _|ALL|_ 

        points on any line of angle $-45^\circ$ have the
        same coordinate sum as one another—such a line is indeed
        the graph of the solution to an equation of the form
        
        $$
        x + y = c
        $$

        (to be read “the coordinate sum is such-and-such [$c$]”)
        because if we solve the latter equation for $y$ we find

        $$
        y = c - x
        $$

        ...a line of slope $-1$, i.e., a line of angle $-45^\circ$!
        (Yes, well, anyway.) In particular, if we draw a “heatmap” of

        $$
        x + y
        $$

        over all points $(x, y) \in \rr^2$ we find a pattern of diagonal bands of 
        slope $-1$: 
        !! where the direction of fastest
        !! increase is up and to the right at $45^\circ$, perpendicularly
        !! to the bands:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/svg_ch5_ex_x_plus_y_rainbow_v2.svg

        !! the most possible towards the red (brown)
        !! zone of the heatmap; this is obviously
        !! $\theta = \eta/2$ (plus or minus any
        !! multiple of $4\eta$):

        !! |> Pause

        !! The maximum value of...

        !! $$
        !! \cos x + \sin x
        !! $$

        !! ...is therefore...

        !! |> Boxed
        !!     style=padding-bottom:0.9em
        !!     $$
        !!     \cos(\eta/2) + \sin(\eta/2) = 2\cdot{1\over \sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{2}
        !!     $$

        !! ...achieved at $x = 45^\circ$ as well as at
        !! $45^\circ$ plus or minus
        !! any multiple of $360^\circ$!

        !! In particular (3rd time's a charm), 
        If we are confined to some region of the plane
        and we need to find a point that maximizes the
        coordinate sum we must go as far up and to the
        right as possible, towards brown—whereas
        to minimize the sum we must go as far down and
        to the left as possible, towards purple!

        Having said this,

        $$
        \cos x + \sin x
        $$

        can be interpreted as the sum of the $x$- and
        $y$-coordinates of the point

        $$
        (\cos x, \sin x)
        $$

        that is a point on the unit circle. In other words,
        the unit circle is “the region of the plane” (cf.
        previous paragraph) to which we are confined—we
        must choose a value of $x$ that puts us as far
        “up and to the right” as possible on the circle.
        That value is... $x = \eta/2$ (!!!!):

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_x_plus_y_eta_over_2.svg

        ...or with any multiple of $4\eta$ added; the _value_
        attained at the maximum is...

        |> Boxed
            style=padding-bottom:0.9em
            $$
            \cos(\eta/2) + \sin(\eta/2) = 2\cdot{1\over \sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{2}
            $$

        ...since $\cos(\eta/2) = \sin(\eta/2) = \sqrt{0.5} = {1\over \sqrt{2}}$.

        |> SolutionNote

            A similar argument shows that the minimum
            of $\cos x + \sin x$ is $-\sqrt{2}$, attained
            at $x = -135^\circ$ plus or minus any multiple
            of $360^\circ$; here is the original graph adorned
            with some of this extra information:

            |> Image
                src=tmp-images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_with_max_min_info.svg

            Or using radians (in some ways clearer):

            |> Image
                src=tmp-images/e5_cosx_plus_sinx_with_max_min_info_radian_version.svg

            (Note that $45^\circ = 0.78539\ldots = \eta/2$
            as a real number, there is no technical difference
            between the two graphs.)
            !! nor any informality committed in the first graph.)

        |> SolutionNote

            Note that...

            $$
            \Large
            \begin{aligned}
            \ {\eta\over 2} + 180^\circ
            \ =&\,\,\up{1.5} {\eta\over 2} + 2\eta\\
            \ =&\,\,\up{1.5} {\eta\over 2} + {4\eta\over 2}\\
            \ =&\,\,\up{1.5} {5\eta\over 2}
            \end{aligned}
            $$

            ...in case you're not familiar with this kind of
            arithmetic!

        |> SolutionNote

            In general, the sum

            $$
            x_0 + y_0
            $$

            of the coordinates of a point $(x, y) \in \rr^2$ 
            affords this geometric interpretation:


            |> Image
                src=tmp-images/svg_ch5_ex_x_plus_y_intersections_x0_y0_ver.svg

            (By the time you reach one of the axes,
            all of the coordinate sum is concentrated
            in one coordinate.)
            This can also be used to compute

            $$
            \cos(\eta/2) + \sin(\eta/2)
            $$

            by the following, somewhat clever diagram:
            !! by the Pythagorean theorem:

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_x_plus_y_pythagoras.svg

            ...which is an application of the Pythagorean theorem. 
            (In the above, $x_0 = \cos(\eta/2)$,
            $y_0 = \sin(\eta/2)$, and the sum is seen to be
            $\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2} = \sqrt{2}$.)

            !! The sum

            !! $$
            !! \cos(\eta/2) + \sin(\eta/2)
            !! $$

            !! can be computed geometrically
